  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 231 PM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nUpper level ridging over the Southeast and Southern Plains will\nstay in place through Friday. A front over Oklahoma will move \nnorthward as a warm front, resulting in a return to hot weather \nThursday and Friday. Maximum temperatures are expected to reach \ninto the mid 90s Thursday and mid to upper 90s to near 100 for \nFriday. The record high for Friday is 101. The upper level ridge\nwill gradually weaken over the weekend and an upper level \ntrough over the western United States is expected to progress \neastward and approach the high plains. There is a small chance \nof t-storms Friday night across west central Kansas as a frontal\nzone sags southward; but most of southwest Kansas will likely \nstay dry. There are better chances for t-storms Saturday evening\nand night given the presence of low level frontal convergence \nunderneath southwesterly mid level flow. T-storm chances linger \ninto Sunday as the upper trough passes across western Kansas. \nClusters of t-storms will push an effective frontal boundary \ninto the southern plains by later Sunday and Monday, with weak \nupslope flow across the high plains. Another upper level trough \nis predicted to approach the central high plains by Monday night\nand Tuesday. The combination of moist upslope flow and \napproaching trough will result in t-storm chances, particularly \nfor far western Kansas. The grand ensemble shows 60-80% chances \nof .5\u201d or greater rainfall across all of central and western \nKansas for the Friday to Tuesday night time frame, which is a \ngood signal for at least modest amounts of beneficial rain. \nGiven the increasing moisture and t-storm clusters over the \nweekend and into early next week, high temperatures will fall \ninto the upper 70s to upper 80s, with the coolest readings near \nI-70 and warmest around Medicine Lodge.",
